  "history": "In Old English, the normal word for bread was hlāf, today's loaf. The word brēad also existed, but it seems to have meant ‘a piece, a bit’. Around 1200, bread became the main word for the food, and loaf was left with its modern meaning: one whole piece of bread. The old word hlāf hides in two surprising places. A lord was once a hlāfweard, a ‘loaf-guard’, the man who kept the bread and fed the people of his house. A lady was a hlǣfdīge, a ‘loaf-kneader’, the woman of the house. Bread itself may come from the same root as brew, because bread rises the way beer bubbles, but this is not certain. German Brot and Dutch brood are relatives.",
  "historyEinfach": "Bread is an old word. But a long time ago, the English word for bread was hlāf. Today we say loaf: a loaf of bread. Old bread meant ‘a small piece’. Later it became the word for the food. There is a nice story in the old word hlāf. A lord was the ‘loaf-guard’. He had the bread and gave it to his people. A lady was the ‘loaf-maker’.",
